t this  this crucial factor of innovation when  people do international comparisons of  healthcare they often look just at life  expectancy or just an infant mortality  or some other measure of the health of  the population and of course you want to  look at those things but you also have  to be looking at the level of innovation  produced in different  countries and the problem is is that the  innovation that the United States  produces we bear the cost for that and  that shows up in our cost statistics and  it's one of the reasons that we end up  spending more than other countries and  yet the benefits of our innovation don't  just show up in our healthcare  statistics it will show up in every  country healthcare statistics or at  least those of the countries that  actually use the innovations that we  create which is most of them especially  the developed countries you looked at a  variety of different ways you know you  can how do you slice innovation you can  slice in a bunch of different ways one  is for instance Nobel Prize winners  correct it turns out that the United  States has had more Nobel Prize winners  in the area of medicine and physiology  then all other countries combined and  this is if you measure it by the  individuals who have received it or the  number of years in the last 40 years or  so that an American has been among the  recipients and another thing you guys  did was look at not just all innovations  but according to a wide survey of  doctors what they regard as the most  important innovations and what did you  find it at regard if you look at the  list of innovations meaning both  treatments and drugs are masuda goals  and diagnostics that doctors tend to  agree are the most important innovations  of the last 30 to 40 years it turns out  that many of those innovations came for  the United States in fact more from the  United States then came from the  European Union and Switzerland combined  what are one or two examples of those  we're ta