y so  are also things that are part of our  advantage when it comes to innovation if  you think about what kind of incentives  the innovators actually need among other  things they need monetary incentives  they need to know that they're going to  get a higher return they're going to get  it sooner and they're going to get it  for a longer period of time well as it  turns out in the United States we use a  great deal of these innovations we tend  to use them faster and we tend to  diffuse their use a lot more quickly as  well the result of that is often that we  end up paying more we both use the  treatments more often and we pay more  for them and this is often regard as a  defect of the u.s. system and perhaps it  is but we have to realize it's not just  bad there's also an upside which is that  it adds to the incentive that innovators  have to create great products that we're  going to be able to use to improve our  lives and then if the u.s. is the model  of how to do it right at least in terms  of innovation you point you and your  co-author point to the UK  in the Nordic nations as places where  innovation is not going so well what are  they doing wrong well among other things  they have centrally organized healthcare  systems that give a great deal of buyer  power bargaining power to the government  to decide what kinds of new treatments  are going to be used how quickly and  what price is going to be paid for them  and basically it's using the power of  government to cram down the expenditures  that are made on health care but at the  same time what they're also doing is  cramming down the amount of innovation  that's going to take place as well when  you have restrictions on the rate of  diffusion of new technologies and how  quickly people are going to be able to  use a technology when it comes available  obviously that's going to reduce the  return that an innovator is going to get  for creating that innovation in the  first place but in some sense they c